## NFT Collection Locking Issue

In Collection, `lockNFTs` enable users to lock their NFTs within a collection, specify the locking duration, and calculate the required credit cost for the operation. However, there is an issue related to the lack of proper validation checks in `ensureMaxLocking` regarding the `vipLevel` required for infinite locking.

## Code Snippet

```solidity
function ensureMaxLocking(uint8 vipLevel, uint256 requireExpiryTs) internal view {
    /// vip level 0 can not use safebox utilities.
    if (vipLevel >= Constants.VIP_LEVEL_COUNT || vipLevel == 0) {
        revert Errors.InvalidParam();
    }
    /// only check when it is not infinite lock
    if (requireExpiryTs > 0) {
        uint256 deltaBucket;
        unchecked {
            deltaBucket = counterStamp(requireExpiryTs) -
            counterStamp(block.timestamp);
        }
        if (deltaBucket == 0 || deltaBucket >
            Constants.getVipLockingBuckets(vipLevel)) {
            revert Errors.InvalidParam();
        }
    }
}
```

There is no explicit check to ensure that only users with a `vipLevel` of seven may perform infinite locking (`expiryTs == 0`), the expected level required to lock NFTs indefinitely. Hence, even users with a `vipLevel` ranging from one to six may perform infinite locking, which is not the intended behavior.

## Remediation

Implement a check to ensure only users with a `vipLevel` of seven may lock NFTs indefinitely.

## Patch

Fixed in commit `4e7c0b8`.

## User Collection Management

In User, collections owned by users are organized as a linked list structure. When a user possesses a key with a locking credit of zero, invoking `recalculateMinMaintCredit` facilitates the removal of the corresponding collection. This function is designed to eliminate empty collections from the linked list, allowing users to tidy up their collections when they no longer hold locked NFTs within them.

## User.sol (SOLIDITY)

```solidity
function recalculateMinMaintCredit(UserFloorAccount storage account) public returns(uint256) {
    [...]
    for (address collection = account.firstCollection; collection != LIST_GUARD && collection != address(0);) {
        CollectionAccount storage tmp = getByKey(account, collection);
        uint256 locking = tmp.totalLockingCredit;
        if (locking == 0) {
            address next = tmp.next;
            removeCollection(account, collection, prev);
            collection = next;
        } else {
            [...]
        }
    }
    [...]
    return maxLocking;
}
```

The vulnerability arises when a user prolongs the locking duration of an NFT key within a collection after removing that collection utilizing `recalculateMinMaintCredit`. Upon extending the key’s locking period, the code fails to automatically reintegrate the associated collection into the linked list. Consequently, the collection is excluded from the list of collections iterated in `recalculateAvailableCredit`.

## Flooring.sol (SOLIDITY)

```solidity
function recalculateAvailableCredit() external returns (uint256 availableCredit) {
    UserFloorAccount storage account = userFloorAccounts[msg.sender];
    availableCredit = account.tokenBalance(feeToken) - account.recalculateMinMaintCredit();
}
```

Thus, it calculates the available credit based on the remaining collections in the linked list. Since the extended collection is not in the linked list, the `account.minMaintCredit` value will revert to its previous value before the key was extended. In other words, the calculation for the extended key’s locking credit is not considered, potentially resulting in an incorrect available credit calculation.

## Flooring Audit 04 | Vulnerabilities

### Proof of Concept

1. User A has a collection (collection A) with one NFT key (Key 1) and a locking credit of 0.
2. User A calls `recalculateMinMaintCredit`, which removes collection A from the linked list because it is empty (no locked NFTs).
3. User A decides to extend the locking period of Key 1 within collection A.
4. At this point, collection A is not automatically added back to the linked list.
5. User A calls `recalculateAvailableCredit` to check their available credit. It does not consider the extended locking credit for Key 1 in collection A because collection A is not part of the linked list.
6. As a result, the `account.minMaintCredit` value reverts to its previous value, failing to account for the extended key’s locking credit.

### Remediation

Ensure when a key is extended, the removed collection is added back.

### Patch

Fixed in `f110fd5`.

## Auction Bucket Management

Auction utilizes buckets to manage and monitor the expiration times of safeBoxes within the application. 

## Function Overview

### `applyDiffToCounters`

The `applyDiffToCounters` function updates a range of counters efficiently by applying a specified difference (diff) to each counter within the range. However, `applyDiffToCounters` does not correctly update the counters within buckets.

```solidity
function applyDiffToCounters(
    CollectionState storage collectionState,
    uint256 startBucket,
    uint256 endBucket,
    int256 diff
) private {
    if (startBucket == endBucket) return;
    uint256[] memory buckets =
        collectionState.countingBuckets.batchGet(startBucket, endBucket);
    unchecked {
        for (uint256 i; i < buckets.length; ++i) {
            if (diff >= 0) {
                buckets[i] = buckets[i] + uint256(diff);
            } else {
                buckets[i] = buckets[i] - uint256(-diff);
            }
        }
    }
    collectionState.countingBuckets.batchSet(startBucket, buckets);
    if (endBucket > collectionState.lastUpdatedBucket) {
        collectionState.lastUpdatedBucket = uint64(endBucket);
    }
}
```

## Issue Description

The issue arises when extending the expiry time of a safeBox upon placing a new bid, which requires an update to counters within buckets. Specifically, when the new expiry time results in bucket reuse (i.e., the new expiry time falls into the same bucket as the old expiry time), `applyDiffToCounters` does not correctly reset the old bucket value. Instead, it increments the old bucket value by one, resulting in inaccurately high bucket values.

## Proof of Concept

Consider the following scenario:

1. Initially, a user A has a safeBox with an expiry time that falls into an old bucket, referred to as bucket A.
2. User A puts it in the auction.
3. User B places a bid, which extends the safeBox’s expiry time; the new expiry time falls into the next bucket, referred to as bucket B.
4. When `applyDiffToCounters` updates the counters, it increments the value of bucket A by one to reflect the extension.
5. However, it does not reset the old bucket value to its original state, meaning it retains the old value.
6. As a result, bucket A now has a value that is higher than it should be.

Thus, any metrics or calculations based on these counters may be incorrect.

## Remediation

Ensure the bucket value is reset to zero in such instances.

## Patch

Fixed in commit `22a7c3d`.

## Auction: prepareSettleRaffles Function

In Auction, `prepareSettleRaffles` checks a list of NFT IDs to determine if each NFT is eligible for settlement based on raffle end times, ticket sales, and safeBox expiration. It returns a compacted list of eligible NFT IDs and the count of eligible NFTs. This information helps perform raffle settlement operations.

## Raffle.sol (SOLIDITY)

```solidity
function prepareSettleRaffles(CollectionState storage collection, uint256[] calldata nftIds)
    public
    returns (bytes memory compactedNftIds, uint256 nftIdsLen)
{
    uint256 nftLen = nftIds.length;
    uint256[] memory tmpNftIds = new uint256[](nftLen);
    uint256 cnt;
    for (uint256 i; i < nftLen;) {
        uint256 nftId = nftIds[i];
        RaffleInfo storage raffle = collection.activeRaffles[nftId];

        if (raffle.endTime >= block.timestamp) {
            revertErrors.ActivityHasNotCompleted();
        }
        if (raffle.isSettling) revert Errors.InvalidParam();
        if (raffle.ticketSold == 0) {
            continue;
        }

        SafeBox storage safeBox = collection.useSafeBox(nftId);
        // raffle must be settled before safebox expired
        // otherwise, it may conflict with auction
        if (safeBox.isSafeBoxExpired()) revert Errors.SafeBoxHasExpire();

        tmpNftIds[cnt] = nftId;
        raffle.isSettling = true;
        unchecked {
            ++cnt;
            ++i;
        }
    }
    [...]
}
```

However, a potential concern arises regarding the interaction of `prepareSettleRaffles` with generating randomness through a verifiable random function request. It is worth noting that any user may invoke `prepareSettleRaffles` multiple times for the same NFT before the verifiable random function relays the random outcome back to the contract.

## Flooring Audit 04 | Vulnerabilities

Each invocation initiates a new randomness request to the verifiable random function, and the user is not subjected to any fees. This implies that users may repeatedly initiate random requests without incurring any expenses, potentially resulting in a griefing attack.

Additionally, we emphasize that re-requesting randomness is generally not advised, as this practice may allow the verifiable random function service provider to withhold fulfillment if the outcome does not align with their preferences. There is also a possibility that miners could manipulate the order of verifiable random function responses on the blockchain.

## Proof of Concept

1. A malicious user identifies an ongoing raffle. The raffle has a considerable prize, and the winner will be determined based on randomness from a verifiable random function.
2. The user exploits the vulnerability by repeatedly invoking `prepareSettleRaffles` for the same NFT associated with the raffle.
3. Each call to `prepareSettleRaffles` triggers a new randomness request to the verifiable random function. Notably, the function does not charge any fees for making these requests.
4. As a result, the user floods the function with multiple requests for the same raffle. Since each request consumes gas, it may result in the drainage of funds from the contract.

## Remediation

Implement a flag set when `prepareSettleRaffles` is called only to be called after the verifiable random function relays the random result back to the contract. Also, charge users for calling the funds required for the function.

## Patch

Fixed in commit `22a7c3d`.

## Issues with the `slice` Function in Array

The `slice` function in Array lacks proper boundary checks for the inputted length. The `slice` function enables the extraction of a portion of an array by specifying the starting index and length of the desired sub-array. However, it does not verify the length parameter.

## Array.sol (SOLIDITY)

```solidity
function slice(uint256[] memory data, uint256 start, uint256 length) internal pure 
    returns (uint256[] memory res) 
{
    res = new uint256[](length);
    unchecked {
        start *= 0x20;
        length *= 0x20;
    }
    for (uint256 i = 0x20; i <= length;) {
        assembly {
            mstore(add(res, i), mload(add(data, add(i, start))))
        }
        unchecked {
            i += 0x20;
        }
    }
}
```

The function utilizes inline assembly to access memory (`mload` and `mstore`) without explicitly checking whether the provided length parameter exceeds the actual length of the data array. As a result, it may attempt to access memory beyond the bounds of the data array, leading to out-of-bounds reads.

## Remediation

Ensure proper validation of the length parameter.

## Patch

Fixed in commit `4df0c0c`.

## Vulnerability Report: Auction Initialization in Vault

While initializing an auction for a specific non-fungible token within the vault, `initAuctionOnVault` removes the incorrect free one. `initAuctionOnVault` sets up the auction for non-fungible tokens stored in a vault, calculates the safe box expiry timestamps, and initializes auction parameters for each one. 

However, in the for loop, when it updates the `freeTokenIds` array by replacing the token’s ID at the current index (`i`) with the last token’s ID in the array, the index utilized is incorrect.

## Code Snippet

```solidity
// Auction.sol
function initAuctionOnVault(
    CollectionState storage collection,
    mapping(address => UserFloorAccount) storage userAccounts,
    address creditToken,
    address collectionId,
    uint256[] memory vaultIdx,
    address bidToken,
    uint96 bidAmount
) public {
    [...]
    /// vaultIdx keeps asc order
    for (uint256 i = vaultIdx.length; i > 0;) {
        unchecked {
            --i;
        }
        [...]
        auctionTemplate.activityId = collection.generateNextActivityId();
        collection.activeAuctions[nftId] = auctionTemplate;
        activityIds[i] = auctionTemplate.activityId;
        collection.freeTokenIds[i] = collection.freeTokenIds[collection.freeTokenIds.length - 1];
        collection.freeTokenIds.pop();
    }
    [...]
}
```

The program replaces `collection.freeTokenIds[i]` with the last element of the array while removing a non-fungible token from the `freeTokenIds` array. Thus, an incorrect index will result in the deletion of the incorrect free one, associating the wrong token with the auction, as the one that should have been removed may remain in the `freeTokenIds` array. The one intended to stay may be removed, causing inconsistency between the actual and intended states of the `freeTokenIds` array, resulting in unexpected behavior during the auction process.

## Remediation

Ensure the code is modified to utilize `vaultIdx[i]` as the index instead of `i`.

## Patch

Fixed in commit `d57c194`.

