summary:
A bug report was submitted by WatchPug and cmichel, which states that the formula of the number of prizes for a degree per the document <https://v4.docs.pooltogether.com/protocol/concepts/prize-distribution/#splitting-the-prizes> is incorrect. The incorrect formula is: 

Number of prizes for a degree = (2^bit range)^degree - (2^bit range)^(degree-1) - (2^bit range)^(degree-2) - ...

The correct formula is either:

Number of prizes for a degree = (2^bit range)^degree - (2^bit range)^(degree-1)

or 

Number of prizes for a degree = 2^(bit range * degree) - 2^(bit range * (degree-1))

Due to the miscalculation of `number of prizes for a degree`, the protocol was giving out more prizes than designed. This was proven by two methods, one using the definition that `degree n` is constructed by 3 chunks, and the other using the formula that `2^x = 1 << x`. 

The recommendation was to change the code from L423-431 at <https://github.com/pooltogether/v4-core/blob/055335bf9b09e3f4bbe11a788710dd04d827bf37/contracts/DrawCalculator.sol#L423-L431> to 

```solidity
if (_prizeTierIndex > 0) {
    return ( 1 << _bitRangeSize * _prizeTierIndex ) - ( 1 << _bitRangeSize * (_prizeTierIndex - 1) );
} else {
    return 1;
}
```

PierrickGT from PoolTogether then confirmed and patched the bug, and Alex the Entreprenerd (judge) commented that the warden found the usage of an incorrect formula that would cause the protocol to give out larger prizes than expected and the sponsor has mitigated in a following PR.

